WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.963
A model is the part of an AI service that produces an answer.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:09.446
Choosing one is like choosing a helper for a particular job.

00:00:09.446 --> 00:00:14.865
A useful first question is what a correct answer to that job would look like.

00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:24.402
Two models can give equally useful answers to a simple question but take different amounts of time or cost different amounts to run.

00:00:24.402 --> 00:00:28.520
A harder question may change which model you would choose.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:34.185
An API is an agreed way for one program to ask another for something.

00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:38.562
Your program sends a request naming a model and a message.

00:00:38.562 --> 00:00:42.459
The service sends back an answer you can inspect.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:45.313
Orbit wants to sort support emails.

00:00:45.313 --> 00:00:48.433
Take a message asking to send boots back.

00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:53.173
Write down the expected label, return, before trying a model.

00:00:53.173 --> 00:00:58.656
Run that message and several other labeled examples through two candidates.

00:00:58.656 --> 00:01:04.950
Keep the messages and instructions the same so the comparison has a clear meaning.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:11.019
Choosing a model means weighing three things, separately for each step your agent takes.

00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:15.774
First, quality: can this model do this step well, every time?

00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:24.232
That matters most for planning, choosing tools and reading tricky policy, and you judge it by testing on real tickets.

00:01:24.232 --> 00:01:28.544
Second, speed: how long can someone wait for the answer?

00:01:28.544 --> 00:01:35.127
A customer in live chat notices a slow reply, while an overnight report does not care.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:41.394
Third, cost: the price per token, times the tokens per call, times the calls per day.

00:01:41.394 --> 00:01:47.623
A small difference per request can matter when the same step runs thousands of times.

00:01:47.623 --> 00:01:49.624
Open one request.

00:01:49.624 --> 00:01:52.456
The model name chooses the candidate.

00:01:52.456 --> 00:01:56.638
The messages contain the customer’s words and the task.

00:01:56.638 --> 00:02:00.194
The output limit bounds how much text may come back.

00:02:00.194 --> 00:02:03.142
A single label needs little space.

00:02:03.142 --> 00:02:10.023
An explanation of several policy rules needs more room and a different way to check quality.

00:02:10.023 --> 00:02:14.880
When the service answers, open the response before judging the result.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:24.408
This service returns a list of content blocks. For a simple reply we are interested in the block containing customer-facing text.

00:02:24.408 --> 00:02:29.904
So we take the first block whose type is text, with one short line of Python.

00:02:29.904 --> 00:02:37.739
Read that text as the customer would. Does it give a complete answer, or does it stop partway through a thought?

00:02:37.739 --> 00:02:45.667
The stop reason tells you why the model stopped: it finished its turn, or it ran into your max tokens ceiling.

00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:53.082
Usage counts the input and output tokens, which you multiply by the price to know what the call cost.

00:02:53.082 --> 00:03:01.478
If the stop reason is max tokens, the reply was cut off, so raise the limit or ask for a shorter answer.

00:03:01.478 --> 00:03:05.982
Read the returned label and compare it with the one you wrote down.

00:03:05.982 --> 00:03:12.066
Record the wait and the reported usage, which tells you how much text was processed.

00:03:12.066 --> 00:03:14.428
Repeat across the examples.

00:03:14.428 --> 00:03:18.205
Choose the helper that meets the actual job’s needs.

00:03:18.205 --> 00:03:26.045
A good email sorter still needs separate testing before you trust it to explain a complicated return.

00:03:26.045 --> 00:03:34.249
Imagine one candidate gets an easy ticket about returns and the second gets a confusing exchange request.

00:03:34.249 --> 00:03:38.838
Their answers tell you very little about which model suits the job.

00:03:38.838 --> 00:03:43.618
Give both candidates the same questions and expected labels.

00:03:43.618 --> 00:03:47.416
Read the first wrong label before counting totals.

00:03:47.416 --> 00:03:54.394
Did the customer explicitly ask to send something back, or was return merely part of a product name?

00:03:54.394 --> 00:03:59.355
That distinction tells you whether the test reflects the work in the queue.

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:05.424
Only after checking the labels compare response times and the amount of text processed.

00:04:05.424 --> 00:04:13.149
A slower candidate might be worth using for a difficult decision while a smaller one handles a routine label.

00:04:13.149 --> 00:04:16.587
Keep the ticket set for the next comparison.
